Sandel then states the day’s program with unusual explicitness. He wants first to defend the narrative conception of the person against the voluntarist conception associated with Kant and Rawls. Then he wants to suggest that if obligations of solidarity are real, that lends force to a further claim: $\text{arguments about justice}
\not\!\!\perp
\text{questions of the good}.$ The rest of the lecture is the working out of that proposal.

