p.src:
The second route is quite different: $\begin{aligned}
\text{non-relativist justice}
&=
\text{rights justified by the moral worth of the ends they serve},\\
\text{case for recognizing a right}
&\Longrightarrow
\text{showing that it honors or advances an important human good}.\end{aligned}$ This does not mean surrendering justice to whatever a community prefers. It means that justice requires us to ask what goods are genuinely worthy of honor, recognition, and protection. In this sense, justice is tied to the good without being reduced to convention.

