The principle can be written as a short derivation: $\begin{aligned}
\text{if } \text{marriage}=\text{procreation only}
&\Longrightarrow
\text{infertile couples may not marry},\\
\text{but infertile couples may marry}
&\Longrightarrow
\text{procreation is not the sole telos of marriage}.\end{aligned}$ This does not by itself settle the question of same-sex marriage. What it does is remove the simplest version of the procreative argument. We are forced to ask whether procreation is the whole point of marriage, or only part of a more complex account.

